  "snippet": "#​673 — November 11, 2026 Web Version 🕒 Fingers crossed, the final release of Postgres 19 lands next week (Thursday, November 19), so we may hold the next issue back a day or two to cover it properly. If next week's news...",
  "coverImage": "https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/ud1tdm04gaspuqkijouh.jpg",
  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​673 — November 11, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192768/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>🕒 Fingers crossed, <strong>the final release of Postgres 19 lands next week</strong> (Thursday, November 19), so we may hold the next issue back a day or two to cover it properly. If next week's newsletter arrives a little later than usual, that's why. :-)<br/>__<br/><em>Your editor, Peter Cooper</em></p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Postgres Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192694/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/ud1tdm04gaspuqkijouh.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192694/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"postgres.ai\">UUIDv4 vs UUIDv7: What a Random Primary Key Costs</a></span> — The difference is minimal if the index fits in memory, but grows rapidly as it outgrows the cache! In a memory-constrained benchmark, UUIDv4 inserts ran at two-thirds the rate of an ordered key <em>and</em> generated more WAL. Nikolay explains why and shares some catches in switching to <code>uuidv7()</code>.</p>\n<p>Nikolay Samokhvalov (PostgresAI) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192693/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/36027d94.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"110\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192693/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.tigerdata.com\">Your Retention Job Shouldn’t Be a Four-Hour DELETE</a></span> — Purging old rows with DELETE bloats the table, hammers WAL, and leaves VACUUM behind. TimescaleDB partitions by time, so retention drops whole chunks in milliseconds. Same Postgres, same SQL. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192693/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Start on Tiger Cloud with a $1000 credit</a>.</p>\n<p>Tiger Data (creators of TimescaleDB) <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #997 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192695/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"stokerpostgresql.blogspot.com\">Is Postgres Really Winning?</a></span> — Postgres is the only top four database gaining ground in <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192696/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">the DB-Engines rankings</a> and might pass SQL Server soon, but it measures 'popularity', not production use. In <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192697/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">part two</a>, Dave looks further afield, at developer surveys, acquisitions, and what AI tools reach for by default. In short, things continue to look bright for Postgres.</p>\n<p>Dave Stokes </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>⏰ <strong>Postgres's quarterly minor releases are due tomorrow</strong>, alongside Postgres 19 RC2. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192698/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">The draft announcement</a> lists 140+ fixes and some post-update reindexing steps. Note that 14.25 will be Postgres 14's final release.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192699/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Xata is joining ClickHouse</a>, whose Managed Postgres service <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192700/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">went GA</a> the same day. Meanwhile, <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192701/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">EDB has acquired Altinity</a>, a major ClickHouse contributor.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Google has unveiled <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192702/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">AlloyDB Express</a>, a scale-to-zero tier of its Postgres-compatible database that spins up in seconds and is aimed at prototyping and coding agents. It's in preview, with a free tier.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🗓️ Tomorrow (November 12, 5pm GMT), there's a <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192703/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">free <em>Postgres Meetup for All</em> online meetup</a> where Adyen's Huseyin Demir will share what went right and wrong upgrading a huge Postgres fleet to v18 with logical replication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇧🇪 The <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192704/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres devroom at FOSDEM 2027</a> takes place on January 30 in Brussels. The CFP is open until November 28.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192705/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"boringsql.com\">Why <code>VACUUM</code> Doesn't Shrink Your Postgres Indexes</a></span> — A page-by-page look, using <code>pageinspect</code>, at why <code>VACUUM</code> thins out B-tree index pages but can't shrink the index, and why sequential keys don't refill the gaps. There's also an <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192706/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">interactive visualizer</a> to try it on a tiny tree yourself.</p>\n<p>Radim Marek </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192707/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"richyen.com\">An Index Scan Is Only Half the Story: How On-Disk Ordering Affects Performance</a></span> — A look at why an identical index scan can become far slower after a migration involving a parallel data load. Richard shares a <code>ctid</code> query to help detect the problem.</p>\n<p>Richard Yen </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192708/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"postgres.ai\">What Postgres is Waiting For (and Why Dashboards Can't Tell You)</a></span> — Wait event dashboards work by sampling, so they can be right about where time goes overall, yet wrong about why one request is slow.</p>\n<p>Dmitry Fomin (PostgresAI) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192709/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">A Database Per Git Worktree That Barely Uses Any Disk Space</a> – Using Postgres 18's <code>file_copy_method = clone</code> on a copy-on-write filesystem like APFS or Btrfs. <cite>Dennis Paagman</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192710/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Our 'Default' <code>postgresql.conf</code>, Step by Step</a> – The baseline Data Egret usually starts with when setting up or taking over a cluster, beginning with connection settings. <cite>Ilya Kosmodemiansky (Data Egret)</cite></p>\n<p>📊 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192711/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Thirteen Small Measurements on Postgres 18</a> – Such as an unindexed foreign key turning a 13ms cascading delete into a 3.6 second one. <cite>Hollis Varden</cite></p>\n<p>📊 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192712/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>json</code> vs <code>jsonb</code>: Size and Speed, Measured</a> <cite>Chris van Eijk</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES AND CODE:</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192713/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/gsdwwaye2plumcpa5nd4.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192713/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PG Observability Map</a> – An interactive map of Postgres's architecture <em>(see above)</em> where you can hover over a component to see which stats views and functions cover it. Inspired by Alexey Lesovsky's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192714/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">pgstats.dev</a> diagram (which seems to be down).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192715/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Supavisor 2.10</a> – Supabase's multi-tenant Postgres connection pooler adds a setting to log or reject session-level <code>SET</code>, <code>LISTEN</code>, advisory locks and temp tables in transaction mode.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192716/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PgQueuer 2.0</a> – A Postgres-backed job queue for Python, built on <code>LISTEN/NOTIFY</code> and <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>. (<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192717/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Docs</a>.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔒 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192718/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pgx 5.12</a> – The popular Postgres driver for Go fixes SQL injection in certain simple-protocol batches and a server-authentication bypass affecting some connection settings.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192719/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pgstream 1.6</a> – Xata's replication and CDC tool fixes a bug where a restart could skip committed transactions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>🔍 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192720/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">A read-only agent for slow Postgres queries</a>. Faster queries without manual triage, bigger instances, or extra replicas. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192720/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Free &amp; open source</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192721/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">WarpBuild</a> infrastructure runs GitHub Actions twice as fast at half the cost. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192721/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Get $50 in free credits</a> with code <code>START50</code>.</p>\n<p>⚡ <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192722/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Rebase</a> is an open-source backend for Postgres: REST, auth, realtime and storage, with your access rules enforced by Postgres RLS.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>AND FINALLY: WHO'S INSIDE THE ELEPHANT?</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192723/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/v7qrulepbkg56zf6otkl.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192723/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.cybertec-postgresql.com\">Don't Dream It, Be It!</a></span> — Slonik, Postgres's mascot, turns up at many Postgres events, but who's in the suit? At last month's PGConf.EU it was Laurenz Albe, who reports that it's <em>hot</em>, you can't see your feet, but everybody wants a photo. His advice to first-time attendees who don't know anyone is… volunteer!</p>\n<p>Laurenz Albe (CYBERTEC) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}